.TH TRANSPLANTE 1 "2026-08-01" "gardien-garde" "Zach's utilities"
.SH NAME
transplante \- relocate a repository so nothing still points at where it was
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B transplante
[\fIlist\fR]
.br
.\" bashify: norun
.B transplante check
\fIrepo\fR \fIdestination\fR
.br
.\" bashify: norun
.B transplante plan
\fIrepo\fR \fIdestination\fR
.br
.B transplante
[\fB\-\-json\fR] [\fB\-q\fR|\fB\-\-quiet\fR] [\fB\-h\fR|\fB\-\-help\fR] [\fB\-\-version\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B transplante
is the constructive half of a move.
.BR fauche (1)
owns the destructive half \- it clears a repository off this host once every
byte of it is recoverable elsewhere \- and until this verb existed nothing
owned the other side. A relocation performed with
.BR mv (1)
alone is the case this verb exists to prevent: the directory arrives, and
every path that named the old location is still naming it.
.PP
.B It moves nothing itself.
.B plan
writes a script to standard output for a human to read and run, so the move
is that human's act, in that human's own shell, at a moment that human chose.
There is no
.B apply
subcommand, and its absence is a decision rather than an omission: a utility
that both judges a relocation safe and performs it has one failure mode with
no survivors. This is
.BR fauche 's
own shape, for the same reason.
.PP
.B It never writes a crontab.
A relocation changes what a schedule points at, but the crontab in this
ecosystem is generated from
.I schedule/*.conf
by scheduler's own
.IR bin/sync\-crontab.sh .
A second writer of that file is a hand\-edit under a new name, so
.B plan
names the regeneration as a step for its reader instead of performing it.
.PP
The condition for relocating is recoverability, never tidiness \- the same
condition
.B fauche
applies, checked before the move rather than after it, because the move is
what makes the old path disappear.
.B check
reports four conditions and one census:
.TP
.B CLEAN
The working tree holds nothing uncommitted or untracked.
.TP
.B PUSHED
Every local commit exists on a remote.
.TP
.B WORKTREES
No registered worktree outside the repository points into its object store.
Moving a repository under a live worktree breaks that worktree, and the
breakage stays silent until the worktree is next used.
.TP
.B OCCUPIED
The destination path is free.
.TP
.B REFERENCES
A count of the files naming the old path, reported per file rather than per
project, because one unresolved reference is enough to strand a live job.
This is a census, not a condition: references do not block a relocation, they
are work the plan hands to its reader.
.PP
References are
.B listed
and never rewritten. A generated substitution across files nobody has read is
how a relocation becomes an outage, so the plan names each file and stops
there.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-\-json
Not wired for any subcommand of this verb. Requesting it is reported rather
than ignored.
.TP
.BR \-q ", " \-\-quiet
Suppress commentary; results only.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print usage and the exit vocabulary.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Print the version.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The promise was kept.
.TP
.B 2
Usage error; the caller is wrong. A relative destination is one of these.
.TP
.B 6
BLIND: it cannot read what it was asked to judge \- a directory that does not
exist, or one that is not a git repository. This is "I cannot see", never
"nothing to report".
.TP
.B 7
REFUSED: the repository cannot be relocated safely as it stands. The refusal
is principled \- this verb does not relocate a repository whose old copy is
not recoverable \- while the condition itself is not permanent. Clearing the
reported rows and asking again is the escalation.
.SH EXAMPLES
Listing the surface costs nothing. It prints bare names, one per line, so a
caller can read it without a parser:
.PP
.RS
.nf
$ transplante
check
plan
.fi
.RE
.PP
A directory that is not a repository is BLIND, never a quiet pass:
.PP
.RS
.nf
$ transplante check /tmp /tmp/elsewhere
transplante: BLIND: /tmp is not a git repository; there is nothing to relocate
transplante: this is "I cannot see", NOT "nothing to report".
$ echo $?
6
.fi
.RE
.PP
A relative destination is the caller's error, not a condition of the
repository:
.PP
.RS
.nf
$ transplante check /tmp relative/path
transplante: destination must be an absolute path (got 'relative/path')
$ echo $?
2
.fi
.RE
.SH COST
Nothing here spends money. This utility reaches no paid service, and it
offers no
.B \-\-summon
flag for any subcommand.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I bin/transplante
This utility.
.TP
.I lib/verb.sh
The shared runtime it sources: the argument grammar, the cost boundary, and
the exit vocabulary. It is this branch's own copy, which is the one its
neighbours in
.I bin/
are written against.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR fauche (1),
.BR mv (1),
.BR git\-worktree (1),
.BR rsync (1)
.PP
Expectations transfer from
.BR mv (1)
for what a relocation is, and from
.BR fauche (1)
for the shape: judge, emit a script, leave the act to a human.
.SH HISTORY
Coined 2026-08-01 by
.B bashify coin
onto a branch that already carried
.B garde
and
.BR fauche ,
under the doctrine that one noun carries many verbs. It was the first verb
coined rather than emitted.
